#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360564 :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂r},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂r}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂r} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +0\\,(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2r{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360565 :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)={y}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $g(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂g}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂g}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂g}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta } & =0\\,(-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )+(2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360566 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{x}^{6}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}(1+{x}^{4})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{x\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}+\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+3{x}^{4}}{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{4}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360567 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle w(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\n$$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂w}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂w}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂w}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂w}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂x}=x,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360568 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+yx$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360570 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}))\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360571 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y}{(x+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+1)-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360572 :question/problem "Given that $h(r,\\theta )=2r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂h}{∂x}=\\frac{∂h}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂r}=2,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂\\theta }=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂x} & =(2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360573 :question/problem "Given that $g(r,\\theta )={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂g}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{∂g}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $g$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂r}=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂\\theta }=-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =r(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360574 :question/problem "Given that $f(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂r}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂r}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂\\theta }=2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{r}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360575 :question/problem "Given that $w(r,\\theta )=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,$ find $\\frac{∂w}{∂y},$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂w}{∂y}=\\frac{∂w}{∂r}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂r}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,\\frac{∂w}{∂\\theta }=-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂w}{∂y} & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{r}(-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360577 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2xz\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $y=t+1$ and $z=t-1$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =[(t+1)^{2}-(t-1)^{2}]\\mathbf{i}+2\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot (t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot (t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(4t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot 1-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}t-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\  & =4+4\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360578 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)={t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz\\operatorname{cos}x)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{sin}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+z\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+y\\operatorname{cos}x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y={e}^{t},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360579 :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y}$ and $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}(xz)+{e}^{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}(xz)+{e}^{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}(xz)+{e}^{y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{1}{z}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,y={t}^{2},$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t}+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360581 :question/problem "Given that $u(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+xyz,$ find $\\frac{∂u}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $u(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂u}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂u}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂u}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $u$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z}=2{x}^{2}z+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂z} & =2{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(2z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360582 :question/problem "Given that $v(x,y,z)=2y-xz,$ find $\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $v(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂v}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂v}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂v}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂v}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $v$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x}=-z,\\,\\frac{∂v}{∂y}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂x} & =-z, \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂v}{∂\\theta } & =-(-z)r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2)r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =rz\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360583 :question/problem "Given that $h(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{x}^{2}{z}^{2},$ find $\\frac{∂h}{∂z},$ where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $h(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ and $z=z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂h}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂z}+\\frac{∂h}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂h}{∂z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $h$ with respect to $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z}=8{x}^{2}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r,\\theta ,$ and $z,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂h}{∂z} & =8{r}^{2}z{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362296 :question/problem "Which of the following regions is connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/ba/ba346990bd792f1e4a9aed0d900ca087315544715a35cd5cd90049c99fdb84ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362309 :question/problem "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:  \n\n![](images/84/848cf1a33161ac9fb25282cadc2f469675198a69b1df4a04d5eb88c15f66ebcc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362310 :question/problem "Which of the regions shown below are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is regions I, II and III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362333 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}=(3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}\\pi y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}\\pi y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf{F}$ is conservative, find the value of $a.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=P(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q(x,y)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field with domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$\\mathbf{F}$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nFor the vector field $\\mathbf{F},$ the domain $D={\\mathbb{R}}^{2},$ and we have \n\n$$\nP=3{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y,\\,Q=ax-\\pi x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y.\n$$\n\nIt's clear that the partial derivatives exist and are continuous on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.$ Equating the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y & =a-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y \\\\ a & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362340 :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}k & -k \\\\ k & k\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{vmatrix}+k\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({k}^{2}+{k}^{2})+(k+5k)+k(k-5k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k+k\\cdot (-4k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-4{k}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{k}^{2}+6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{k}^{2}+6k & =0 \\\\ k(-2k+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362379 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of  $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is $-6$, find the value of $n.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4-n\\cdot 2+0 \\\\  & =4-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-6,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2n & =-6 \\\\ -2n & =-10 \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362486 :question/problem "A parametrization of the cylindrical heater is $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta ,z)=⟨$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩,$ where $(\\theta ,z)\\in C.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the circle\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1\n$$\n\nin the $xy$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨x,y⟩=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),\n$$\n\nsince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{9}+\\frac{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{9} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(\\theta ,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }},\\hspace{0.167em}3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,\\hspace{0.167em}z⟩\\end{aligned}$$\n\nwhere $(\\theta ,z)\\in C$ and $C=[0,2\\pi )\\times [0,3].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362489 :question/problem "A heating element $E$ lies on the intersection of the cylindrical heater and the plane $z=2.$ Use your answer to part 4 to find an outward-pointing unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}(x,y)$ to $E$ that lies within this plane. Express your answer in terms of $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: The matrix ${R}_{z}(ϕ)$ representing a counterclockwise rotation of $ϕ$ radians about the $z$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\n{R}_{z}(ϕ)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ & \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$\\mathbf{n}(x,y)=\\hspace{0.222em},$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\hspace{0.167em},$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\hspace{0.222em}+$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$" :question/worked-solution "We found that a parametrization of the cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta ,z)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,z⟩\\hspace{2em}(\\theta ,z)\\in C.\n$$\n\nRestricting the parametrization of the cylinder to $z=2,$ we get a parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(\\theta )$ for the heating element:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(\\theta )=\\mathbf{f}(\\theta ,2)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ,3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ,2⟩\n$$\n\nFor convenience, we express $\\mathbf{r}(\\theta )$ in column vector column:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(\\theta )=\\begin{bmatrix}x(\\theta ) \\\\ y(\\theta ) \\\\ z(\\theta )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \nNotice that this curve is traversed *counterclockwise* when viewed from above.\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing unit normal vector to this curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(\\theta ).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(\\theta )=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(\\theta ) \\\\ {y}^{'}(\\theta ) \\\\ {z}^{'}(\\theta )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Since $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(\\theta )$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians clockwise $(ϕ=-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nUsing the hint, the rotation matrix corresponding to a $ϕ=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ counterclockwise rotation about the $z$-axis is\n\n$$\n{R}_{z}(\\theta )=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(\\theta )$ by $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{\\mathbf{n}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we normalize the vector found in Step 2:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||\\overset{~}{\\mathbf{n}}|| & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{\\mathbf{n}}}{||\\overset{~}{\\mathbf{n}}||} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}x(\\theta ) \\\\ y(\\theta ) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362496 :question/problem "Water flows over the element $E$ according to the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=6y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nSince the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $E$ found in part 5 has zero $\\mathbf{k}$-component, it may be considered a two-dimensional vector defined in terms of $\\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{j}$ only. Use this to find an expression for the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the element in terms of $x$ and $y.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Flux = \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{E}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $E$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $E$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{E}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit vector to $E.$\n\n![](images/2c/2cd5e4c7ab86cdb999e239e986713a51c07b8decb629b1ee4a656077590f04bb.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{E}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{E}(6y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{x}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}2xy+\\frac{1}{3}xy\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}\\frac{7}{3}xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362505 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down a double integral that gives the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C,$ transversed counterclockwise.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em},\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/46/4693916207540a6453c6c3775824ba577e62fea0a18b07e96f8814901d9ad80e.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-2{y}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+(-2xy)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=-2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\hspace{0.167em}0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2y-(-4y))\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\hspace{0.222em}\\mathrm{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362524 :question/problem "$\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{G}=\\hspace{0.222em}$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{G}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{i}}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{j}}+R\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{\\text{k}}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{G}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{G}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{G}=\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{G}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{G} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}+\\frac{∂R}{∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-xy-2{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362533 :question/problem "$\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}\\hspace{0.167em}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the divergence of $\\mathbf{F}$ is the scalar field given by\n\n$$\n\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F}=\\mathrm{∇}\\cdot \\mathbf{F}=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{F} & =\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =3{y}^{2}+(-3{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362536 :question/problem "The scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{y}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0-(3x\\cdot 2y) \\\\  & =-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362539 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to write down the integral that gives the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along the border $C$ of the plot $D,$ shown in the diagram above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nCirculation $$\n\\displaystyle \\,={∮}_{C}^{}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={∬}_{D}\\,\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y={∬}_{D}\\underbrace{(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})}_{\\text{Scalar curl of }\\mathbf{F}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}={∮}_{C}\\underbrace{(3x{y}^{2})}_{P}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x+\\underbrace{(-{y}^{3})}_{Q}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nIn part 2, we found that the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-6xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}-6xy\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362542 :question/problem "Write down the double integral from the previous part in polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A\\,={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{a}\\,{\\int }_{0}^{b}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \nwhere $a\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nand $b\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-3}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-6xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}-6(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362552 :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfbc570ba4fc6bd4923edc2d0efae0e735e671f9b4350226159e591e0bdae6c7.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nThe net $L$ is represented by the line segment shown in the diagram above. \nCalculate the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through $L,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n{\\int }_{L}^{\\phantom{C}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s\\hspace{0.167em}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s={\\int }_{C}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n* the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n* the unit normal vector $\\mathbf{n}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf{T}(t).$\n\nParameterizing the line segment $L,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{2}t,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}t,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the line from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(\\sqrt{2},-\\sqrt{2}).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{n}$ is indeed a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf{T},$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =3x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}t)(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =-(-\\sqrt{2}t)^{3} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}t)=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{2}t)=-\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{L}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{L}P\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}y-Q\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (-\\sqrt{2})-(2\\sqrt{2}{t}^{3})\\cdot (\\sqrt{2})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-12{t}^{3}-4{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-16{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4[{t}^{4}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4(1-0) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362555 :question/problem "![](images/83/8354a964dc235f1c9676754cf7d163cb4a54f05bd5899ff398acb11a4287861d.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\nLet $∂R$ be the boundary of the region $R,$ shown above. Use Green's theorem to calculate the following integral:\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}^{\\phantom{C}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\\hspace{0.167em}={∮}_{∂R}^{\\phantom{C}}3x{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}x-{y}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathrm{d}y\\hspace{0.167em}=\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nAs we saw earlier, we can use Green's theorem to write our line integral in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{∂R}3x{y}^{2}\\,dx-{y}^{3}\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nBut this time, our region $R$ (in polar coordinates) is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}1\\le r\\le 2,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{Δ}-3{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3{[\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{-2}]}_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{r=1}^{r=2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}({2}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362860 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+1\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (2x+1)\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(2x+1){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{9}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x+1){e}^{3x}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362867 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{4x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4}-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4}-{\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4(1/4)}-0\\cdot {e}^{4\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{1}{16}[{e}^{4(1/4)}-{e}^{4\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{e}{4}-0]-\\frac{1}{16}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}-\\frac{e}{16}+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362996 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x+5}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363003 :question/problem "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363031 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy=3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3} \\\\ xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{t}(-1,-1)={f}_{y}(-1,-1)\\mathbf{i}-{f}_{x}(-1,-1)\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $\\mathbf{t}(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =(x+3{y}^{2})i-(y+2x)j \\\\ t(-1,-1) & =(-1+3(-1)^{2})i-(-1+2(-1))j \\\\  & =2\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.$"}]}
